      "content": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <math.h>\n\n#define N_ITER 10 // number of simulation iterations\n#define DT 0.01f // time step\n#define SOFTENING 1e-9f // to avoid zero divisors\n#define BI 8       /* i-tile size for register blocking */\n\n/*\n * Each body holds coordinate positions (i.e., x, y, and z) and\n * velocities (i.e., vx, vy, and vz).\n */\ntypedef struct { float x, y, z, vx, vy, vz; } Body;\n\n/*\n * Compute the gravitational impact among all pairs of bodies in \n * the system.\n *\n * The force loop is register-blocked over i: for each tile of BI\n * bodies we preload their coordinates into local arrays and then\n * sweep j from 0 to n-1, reusing each loaded p[j] across the whole\n * tile. This reduces memory traffic by a factor of BI while keeping\n * the per-body summation order (j increasing) bit-exact.\n */\nvoid body_force(Body *restrict p, float dt, int n) {\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int i0 = 0; i0 < n; i0 += BI) {\n    int count = (i0 + BI <= n) ? BI : (n - i0);\n\n    float px[BI], py[BI], pz[BI];\n    float fx[BI], fy[BI], fz[BI];\n\n    for (int ii = 0; ii < count; ++ii) {\n      int idx = i0 + ii;\n      px[ii] = p[idx].x;\n      py[ii] = p[idx].y;\n      pz[ii] = p[idx].z;\n      fx[ii] = 0.0f;\n      fy[ii] = 0.0f;\n      fz[ii] = 0.0f;\n    }\n\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n      float pjx = p[j].x;\n      float pjy = p[j].y;\n      float pjz = p[j].z;\n\n      #pragma omp simd\n      for (int ii = 0; ii < count; ++ii) {\n        float dx = pjx - px[ii];\n        float dy = pjy - py[ii];\n        float dz = pjz - pz[ii];\n        float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n        float inv_dist = 1 / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n        float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n\n        fx[ii] += dx * inv_dist3; \n        fy[ii] += dy * inv_dist3; \n        fz[ii] += dz * inv_dist3;\n      }\n    }\n\n    for (int ii = 0; ii < count; ++ii) {\n      int idx = i0 + ii;\n      p[idx].vx += dt*fx[ii];\n      p[idx].vy += dt*fy[ii];\n      p[idx].vz += dt*fz[ii];\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/*\n * Read a binary dataset with initilized bodies.\n */\nBody* read_dataset(int *nbodies) {\n  int b = fread(nbodies, sizeof(*nbodies), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading nbodie value\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  Body *bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n\n  return bodies;\n}\n\n/*\n * Write simulation results into a binary dataset.\n */\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n\n  Body *bodies = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  /*\n   * At each simulation iteration, interbody forces are computed,\n   * and bodies' positions are integrated.\n   */\n  for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; iter++) {\n    body_force(bodies, DT, nbodies);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; i++) {\n      bodies[i].x += bodies[i].vx * DT;\n      bodies[i].y += bodies[i].vy * DT;\n      bodies[i].z += bodies[i].vz * DT;\n    }\n  }\n\n  write_dataset(nbodies, bodies);\n\n  free(bodies);\n  \n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n",
      "path": "src/nbody.c"
    }
  ],
  "hypothesis": "Register-blocking the i-loop (tile size BI=8) with OpenMP parallelization over tiles reduces memory traffic by reusing each loaded j-body's coordinates across a tile of i bodies, while preserving the exact per-i summation order for bit-identical output. Preloading the tile's own coordinates into local arrays further cuts repeated loads of p[i], improving cache locality and speed on 4 cores.",
  "notes": "The transformation keeps all arithmetic expressions identical to the original (including double-precision sqrt) and preserves the j-increasing accumulation order per body, so output bytes are unchanged. OpenMP static scheduling distributes independent i-tiles across threads; no cross-tile data dependencies exist. The inner ii loop is marked omp simd because iterations write disjoint accumulators, enabling safe vectorization without altering summation order.",
